==============================================================================
TEST 1  homogeneous limit -- must reproduce strain_fourier (independent method)
==============================================================================
  The two methods discretize differently (exact operator on a sampled mask vs Q1 finite
  elements), so they do not have to agree at finite h -- but the difference must SHRINK
  with h, since both converge to the same continuum solution. A constant offset would
  mean one of them is solving a different problem.

  shape             h   grid  fourier Tr       FD Tr |diff|/|Tr|   CG
  pyramid b=10  1.000     32   -0.088272   -0.094889       7.50%    1
  pyramid b=10  0.500     64   -0.088117   -0.091502       3.84%    1
  pyramid b=10  0.333     96   -0.087850   -0.089240       1.58%    1
  sphere R=6    1.000     32   -0.092603   -0.095554       3.19%    1
  sphere R=6    0.500     64   -0.092550   -0.094175       1.76%    1
  sphere R=6    0.333     96   -0.092495   -0.093552       1.14%    1
  CG converges in one iteration in the homogeneous limit: the preconditioner is the
  EXACT inverse of the homogeneous operator, so there is nothing left for CG to do.
  That is a strong check on the symbol, the stiffness and the connectivity at once.

